sure that we only proceed if there is actual content in the returned tuple.\n\nFile: `src/requests/utils.py`\n\n```python\n        try:\n            _netrc = netrc(netrc_path).authenticators(host)\n            if _netrc:\n                # Return with login / password\n                login_i = 0 if _netrc[0] else 1\n                return (_netrc[login_i], _netrc[2])\n        except (NetrcParseError, OSError):\n```\n\nI will change `if _netrc:` to check if the tuple is not empty and if its elements are not all empty strings, or more simply, check if the tuple is not `('', '', '')`. Since `netrc` returns a tuple of strings, checking if the tuple is non-empty is not enough.\n\nA robust check would be:\n```python\n            _netrc = netrc(netrc_path).authenticators(host)\n            if _netrc and any(c for c in _netrc):\n                # Return with login / password\n                login_i = 0 if _netrc[0] else 1\n                return (_netrc[login_i], _netrc[2])\n```\nHowever, to keep the change minimal and targeted, I will check if the tuple is not empty and if the first element is not empty, as that usually indicates a login attempt. If the tuple is `('', '', '')`, `_netrc[0]` is `''`.\n\nLet's use a check that ensures at least one of the components is non-empty, or simply check if the tuple is not `('', '', '')`.\n\nIf I use `if _netrc and any(c for c in _netrc):`, this covers the case where `_netrc` is `('', '', '')` because `any(c for c in ('', '', ''))` is `False`.\n\nI will apply this change.\n\n```python\nfilepath:<|\"|>src/requests/utils.py<|\"|>,old_string:<|\"|>            if _netrc:\n                # Return with login / password\n                login_i = 0 if _netrc[0] else 1\n                return (_netrc[login_i], _netrc[2])<|\"|>,new_string:<|\"|>            if _netrc and any(c for c in _netrc):\n                # Return with login / password\n                login_i = 0 if _netrc[0] else 1\n                return (_netrc[login_i], _netrc[2])<|\"|>,allow_multiple:false\n```<channel|>", "error_code": null, "error_message": null}
